      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Acute aortic dissection coexisting with coronary malperfusion is a relatively rare but fatal condition. Surgical treatment of these patients is to perform early coronary revascularization concomitant with aortic repair. We review our surgical results of a selected group of 14 patients with type A acute aortic dissection and coronary artery dissection."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Between January 1993 and March 2005, 14 patients (10.2%) from a total of 136 consecutive patients with acute type A aortic dissection concomitant coronary dissection were treated by performing aortic repair and coronary artery bypass grafting. There were 11 men and 3 women (mean age, 56.7 +/- 8.4 years). The right coronary artery was involved in eight patients, the left in two patients, and both coronary arteries in four patients. At admission, nine patients had Q waves (64.2%), inferior in seven (50%) and anterior or lateral in two (14.2%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Hospital mortality rate was 21.4% (3 of 14 patients). Of these, two patients could not be weaned from cardiopulmonary bypass, and one patient died of multiorgan failure in the intensive care unit."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Since acute type A aortic dissection with coronary involvement is associated with high mortality rate, immediate coronary artery bypass grafting and aortic repair is a safe and reliable approach to these challenging group of patients."
        }
      ]},
